# Wave Communication Showcase

The Wave showcase demonstrates JRebirth's inter-model messaging system.
It shows how two models — running in different `Stage` windows — can communicate through waves without holding direct references to each other.
This is one of the simplest and most instructive showcases: the entire application is just a handful of files, but it illustrates the foundational messaging pattern that every JRebirth application relies on.


## Application Entry Point

The application is minimal — a single `DefaultApplication` subclass that points to `MainModel`.

]]#


```java
package org.jrebirth.af.showcase.wave;

import javafx.scene.layout.StackPane;

import org.jrebirth.af.api.ui.Model;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.application.DefaultApplication;
import org.jrebirth.af.showcase.wave.ui.main.MainModel;

public class WaveApplication extends DefaultApplication<StackPane> {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        launch(args);
    }

    @Override
    public Class<? extends Model> firstModelClass() {
        return MainModel.class;
    }

}
```

#[[

No stylesheets, no resource preloading — just the bare minimum to launch the first model.


## Defining Wave Types — WWaves

Before any model can send or receive waves, the wave types must be declared.

]]#


```java
package org.jrebirth.af.showcase.wave.ui;

import org.jrebirth.af.api.annotation.Preload;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.contract.WaveType;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.wave.WBuilder;

@Preload
public interface WWaves {

    String CHANGE = "CHANGE";

    WaveType CHANGE_WT = WBuilder.waveType(CHANGE);

}
```

#[[

This interface defines a single wave type: `CHANGE_WT`.
The `@Preload` annotation ensures that the static fields are initialized before any model starts listening, avoiding class-loading race conditions.

The `CHANGE` string constant serves as the wave's action name.
By convention, when a model listens for this wave type, JRebirth will route it to a method named `doChange(Wave)`.
The `@OnWave` annotation provides an alternative, annotation-driven approach.


## MainModel — Receiving Waves with @OnWave

The main model uses an FXML-based view and listens for incoming `CHANGE` waves.

]]#


```java
package org.jrebirth.af.showcase.wave.ui.main;

import org.jrebirth.af.api.module.Register;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.ui.ModuleModel;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.Wave;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.annotation.OnWave;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.ui.fxml.DefaultFXMLModel;
import org.jrebirth.af.showcase.wave.ui.WWaves;

@Register(value = ModuleModel.class)
public class MainModel extends DefaultFXMLModel<MainModel> implements ModuleModel {

    @OnWave(WWaves.CHANGE)
    protected void whatyouwant(Wave wave) {
        getFXMLController().txtMessage.setText("Wave received : " + wave.waveType().action());
    }

    @Override
    public String moduleName() {
        return "Wave";
    }

    @Override
    public MainController getFXMLController() {
        return (MainController) super.getFXMLController();
    }

}
```

#[[

### How @OnWave works

The `@OnWave(WWaves.CHANGE)` annotation on `whatyouwant()` tells the framework:
"When a wave with action `CHANGE` arrives, call this method."

The method name does **not** need to follow the `doXxx` convention when using `@OnWave` — you can name it anything.
Inside the handler, the model updates the FXML controller's text field to show that the wave was received.

Notice that `MainModel` extends `DefaultFXMLModel` — the view is defined in an FXML file, and the controller is an `AbstractFXMLController` subclass.
The `getFXMLController()` override provides typed access to the controller.


## MainController — Opening a Second Stage

The FXML controller creates and shows a second window containing `SecondModel`.

]]#


```java
package org.jrebirth.af.showcase.wave.ui.main;

import org.jrebirth.af.api.ui.View;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.ui.fxml.AbstractFXMLController;
import org.jrebirth.af.showcase.wave.ui.second.SecondModel;

import javafx.event.ActionEvent;
import javafx.scene.Parent;
import javafx.scene.Scene;
import javafx.scene.control.TextField;
import javafx.stage.Stage;

public final class MainController extends AbstractFXMLController<MainModel, View<MainModel, ?, ?>> {

    public TextField txtMessage;

    private Stage stage;

    public void onOk(ActionEvent actionEvent) {
        if (stage == null) {
            stage = new Stage();
            stage.setScene(new Scene((Parent) model().getModel(SecondModel.class).node(), 600, 400));
        }
        stage.show();
    }

}
```

#[[

When the user clicks the "OK" button in the main window, `onOk` creates a new `Stage` and populates it with `SecondModel`'s node.
The key line is:

```java
model().getModel(SecondModel.class).node()
```

This retrieves (or creates) the `SecondModel` singleton through JRebirth's facade and obtains its root JavaFX node.
The second model lives in a different `Stage`, but it is managed by the **same** JRebirth facade — which is why waves can flow between them.


## SecondModel — Listening and Logging

The secondary model listens for the same `CHANGE_WT` wave type and logs when it receives one.

]]#


```java
package org.jrebirth.af.showcase.wave.ui.second;

import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.Wave;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.ui.fxml.DefaultFXMLModel;
import org.jrebirth.af.showcase.wave.ui.WWaves;

public class SecondModel extends DefaultFXMLModel<SecondModel> {

    @Override
    protected void initModel() {
        listen(WWaves.CHANGE_WT);
    }

    public void doChange(final Wave wave) {
        System.out.println("SecondPageModel " + wave.waveType().action());
    }
}
```

#[[

Here the listening is done imperatively in `initModel()` via `listen(WWaves.CHANGE_WT)`.
By convention, when a wave with action `"CHANGE"` arrives, JRebirth calls `doChange(Wave)`.

This demonstrates the **two ways to listen for waves**:

| Approach | Example | Naming rule |
|----------|---------|-------------|
| `@OnWave("ACTION")` on a method | `MainModel.whatyouwant()` | Any method name |
| `listen(waveType)` in `initModel()` | `SecondModel.doChange()` | Must be `doXxx` matching the action |

Both work identically — `@OnWave` is more declarative, while `listen()` is useful when you need to conditionally subscribe.


## SecondController — Sending Waves

The second window's controller sends waves back to the main model.

]]#


```java
package org.jrebirth.af.showcase.wave.ui.second;

import javafx.event.ActionEvent;

import org.jrebirth.af.api.ui.View;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.ui.fxml.AbstractFXMLController;
import org.jrebirth.af.showcase.wave.ui.WWaves;
import org.jrebirth.af.showcase.wave.ui.main.MainController;
import org.jrebirth.af.showcase.wave.ui.main.MainModel;

public class SecondController extends AbstractFXMLController<SecondModel, View<SecondModel, ?, ?>> {

    public void onSayHello(ActionEvent actionEvent) {
        final MainController controller = model().getModel(MainModel.class).getFXMLController();
        controller.txtMessage.setText("Hello, world");
    }

    public void onButton2(ActionEvent actionEvent) {
        model().sendWave(WWaves.CHANGE_WT);
    }
}
```

#[[

Two buttons demonstrate two different communication patterns:

### Direct model access — `onSayHello`

```java
model().getModel(MainModel.class).getFXMLController().txtMessage.setText("Hello, world");
```

This reaches across models via the facade, gets the `MainModel`, and directly modifies its controller's UI field.
While this works, it creates a **tight coupling** between the two models — `SecondController` must know about `MainModel`'s internal controller structure.

### Wave-based communication — `onButton2`

```java
model().sendWave(WWaves.CHANGE_WT);
```

This sends a `CHANGE` wave into the JRebirth message bus.
Any model that is listening for `CHANGE_WT` will receive it — `MainModel` (via `@OnWave`) and `SecondModel` itself (via `listen()`), both.

The sender does not know or care who is listening.
This is the **recommended** approach: loosely coupled, testable, and extensible.


## Multi-Stage Communication

A key takeaway from this showcase is that **waves cross Stage boundaries transparently**.
The main model and the second model live in different JavaFX `Stage` windows, but they share the same JRebirth facade.
When `SecondController` sends a wave, it reaches `MainModel` in the other window without any special plumbing.

This makes waves ideal for building multi-window applications — tool windows, floating panels, preferences dialogs — where components need to communicate without knowing each other's window hierarchy.


## Key Patterns Summary

| Pattern | Where | Why |
|---------|-------|-----|
| `@Preload` on wave interface | `WWaves` | Ensure wave types are initialized before models start |
| `@OnWave` annotation | `MainModel` | Declarative, name-independent wave listening |
| `listen()` + naming convention | `SecondModel` | Imperative wave subscription with `doXxx` dispatch |
| `sendWave()` | `SecondController` | Fire-and-forget decoupled messaging |
| `DefaultFXMLModel` | `MainModel`, `SecondModel` | FXML-backed models with typed controller access |
| Cross-Stage waves | Entire app | Transparent messaging between windows via shared facade |

]]#
